findings.body:
The guard that rejects `userIds` outside the caller's scope used to allow the request only when the filter was exactly `[me]`. The PR changes it to `filters.userIds.includes(user.id)`. Now `isCurrentUser` is true whenever the caller's own id appears anywhere in the list, and `areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam && !isCurrentUser` short-circuits the throw.

Attack: a team ADMIN sends `userIds: [myId, victimId]` where `victimId` belongs to another team. `isCurrentUser` is true, so no FORBIDDEN is thrown. Query 1 then runs `Booking.userId IN (myId, victimId)`, and query 2 runs `Attendee.email IN (victim email)` from `attendeeEmailsFromUserIdsFilter`. The victim's bookings come back.

The unit tests don't cover a mixed list, so this passes CI.

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="144-159"}

::callout{type="security"}
Restore the original "only me" semantics. The membership check should accept a list only when every id is the caller or is in scope. Don't treat the caller's presence as a bypass for the rest of the list.

::suggestion{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="145-154"}
const areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam = filters.userIds.every(
  (userId) => userId === user.id || allAccessibleUserIds.includes(userId)
);

if (!areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam) {
  throw new TRPCError({
    code: "FORBIDDEN",
    message: "You do not have permissions to fetch bookings for specified userIds",
  });
}

`PermissionRepository.getTeamIdsWithPermission` now accepts `orgId`, but the body still calls the plural method without it:

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="211-223"}

The handler calls this singular method with `orgId: user.orgId ?? undefined`, so the SQL predicates `${orgId}::bigint IS NULL OR ...` always get `NULL`/`undefined`. Every team where the caller has `booking.read` or ADMIN/OWNER is returned, across all orgs.

Before this PR, the membership query was restricted to `user.orgId` and its child teams. An ADMIN of org B who is viewing org A now sees org B members' bookings and attendee emails. The PR's stated purpose is to fix this scope, so the fix is incomplete.

The integration test passes `orgId` to the plural method directly, so it never reaches this bug.

::callout{type="warning"}
Forward `orgId` in the singular method. This is a one-line fix plus a regression test that calls the singular method with a non-null `orgId`.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="222-222"}
    return this.getTeamIdsWithPermissions({ userId, permission, fallbackRoles, orgId });

`getTeamIdsWithPermissions` forwards `orgId` straight into the helper queries, which interpolate it into `${orgId}::bigint IS NULL OR t."id" = ${orgId}`. For any caller without an org, that value is `undefined` (the handler passes `user.orgId ?? undefined`).

Prisma's tagged-template serialization of `undefined` isn't something I'd rely on. If it throws, `PermissionCheckService` catches it, logs, and returns `[]`. A user without an org would then see only their own bookings, with no error reaching the caller. I couldn't run the test suite to confirm which way it goes, so this is a risk to verify. Either way, the code should normalize the value explicitly.

::diff{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="246-250"}

The fix is to normalize to `null` before the queries run. The helper signatures already accept `number | null`.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="248-251"}
    const orgScope = orgId ?? null;
    const [teamsWithPermission, teamsWithFallbackRoles] = await Promise.all([
      this.getTeamsWithPBACPermissions(userId, permissionPairsJson, permissions.length, orgScope),
      this.getTeamsWithFallbackRoles(userId, fallbackRoles, orgScope),
    ]);

Three tests end with `expect(promise).resolves.not.toThrow()`. Vitest's `toThrow` is for functions, and after `.resolves` it doesn't check that the resolved bookings value has any particular shape. The assertion passes for almost any resolved value, including an empty result or a wrong one.

The first case, "should allow access when filtering by own userId", is the one that should guard the `isCurrentUser` relaxation, and it never checks the query or its result. The fallback-role and combined-role cases have the same weakness.

Replace these with assertions on observable output: the bookings returned, or the `where` clause that reached `kysely`. Also add a case with a mixed list like `[self, outsider]` that expects FORBIDDEN. Right now that case is untested and passes the bypass described in the first finding.

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.test.ts" lines="249-277"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The assertion at line 277 can't fail. The same pattern appears at lines 299 and 333.

The only handler test that checks the `PermissionCheckService` call uses `mockUser` with `orgId: null`. `getBookings` then passes `orgId: undefined`, so the assertion `toHaveBeenCalledWith({ userId, permission, fallbackRoles })` matches whether or not `orgId` is forwarded. The repository's dropped `orgId` (finding above) would pass this test too.

Add a case with `orgId` set (for example 7) and assert the service receives `orgId: 7`. Without it, a regression in the call site stays invisible.

::code{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.test.ts" lines="249-260"}

The diff adds `import { TRPCError } from "@trpc/server";` to `permission-check.service.ts`, but nothing in the file uses it. Lint will flag it, and this repo's CI treats lint warnings as failures.

The import is probably left over from an earlier draft that threw errors from the service. The service's existing pattern is to catch and return `[]`, so the import should go.

::diff{file="packages/features/pbac/services/permission-check.service.ts" lines="5-8"}

The two `orgId` predicates aren't symmetric. In the PBAC query's child branch, the check is `org.id = orgId OR child.id = orgId`. In the fallback child branch it's `org.id = orgId OR child.id = orgId OR child.parentId = orgId`.

Since the join already sets `child.parentId = org.id`, the extra `child.parentId = orgId` is just `org.id = orgId` again, so it does nothing. The mismatch makes it hard to tell whether the scope rule is meant to differ between the two paths. Pick one predicate shape and use it in both branches.

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="346-357"}
